---
// Homepage. Ported from design/10xgraph/v4-runtime.html (v3 layout with the premium white CTA and
// cobalt accent); the hero stage comes from design/10xgraph/hero/b-isometric.html. Styles live in src/styles/home.css, scoped to .home. This file is now the source of
// truth for the homepage; edit it here, not in the design prototype.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/home.css';

const title = "10xGraph: Python Multi-Agent Framework With Built-In Server";
const description = "Open-source Python multi-agent framework. Write the agent and 10xGraph generates its production server: auth, rate limits, replay-safe tools and Kubernetes.";
// Single source for the visible FAQ and the FAQPage JSON-LD, so the two never drift apart.
// Ordered to lead with the generated API server: you write the agent, 10xGraph serves it.
const faq = [
  {
    q: "What is 10xGraph?",
    a: "10xGraph is an open-source Python framework for production multi-agent AI. You write the agent: the graph, the tools and the prompts. 10xGraph builds and runs the API server around it, with streaming endpoints, authentication, owner-only threads, rate limits, persistent memory, and Docker and Kubernetes deployment files. It is MIT licensed and made by 10xScale."
  },
  {
    q: "What do I write, and what does 10xGraph build for me?",
    a: "You write agent.py: a StateGraph or the prebuilt ReactAgent, your tools and your system prompt. 10xGraph builds everything between that file and your users: REST, SSE and WebSocket endpoints, JWT or custom authentication, owner-only threads and scoped permissions, rate limits backed by memory or Redis, prompt-injection validators, thread state in Redis and PostgreSQL, and a Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml and Kubernetes manifests."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need to write FastAPI or any other server code?",
    a: "No. You write the agent code, and you decide how the server behaves in production through one file, 10xgraph.json. Run 10xgraph config to open it in a browser editor, then choose authentication, authorization, rate limits, the memory store and the environment file. Every field is checked with the same parser the server uses, and secrets stay in your .env file. Run 10xgraph dev and the server starts on port 8000 with a playground UI, serving invoke, stream, thread, memory and file endpoints. When you change the config, the server adjusts. When you change the graph, the endpoints stay the same."
  },
  {
    q: "Is the API server production-ready?",
    a: "Yes. 10xScale runs its own AI products on it. Each part of running agents in production has its own options, and most of them are a key in 10xgraph.json or an environment variable. You tune production behavior without touching your agent code.\n\nSecurity: authentication is JWT or your own BaseAuth class. Authorization is owner-only threads, allow-all, or your own backend. MODE=production refuses to start with wildcard CORS plus credentials, rejects JWT secrets shorter than 32 bytes, and turns off the /docs pages by default. Security headers are on by default. The production template adds a prompt-injection validator and lifecycle validators that run before invoke, after each step and on error.\n\nReliability: tool calls are replay-safe after a crash, and versioned state writes stop two runs from overwriting each other. Node and tool timeouts stop a hung call from holding a worker. Thread state is cached in Redis and stored durably in PostgreSQL, so threads survive restarts.\n\nScale: rate limits use a sliding window keyed by IP, by user or globally, and they are kept in the API's cache store, in memory or in Redis so every worker shares them, or in your own store. The server runs Gunicorn with Uvicorn workers, and you can run several instances behind a load balancer as long as they share PostgreSQL and Redis. WebSocket connection limits, upload size limits and allowed file types are configurable.\n\nDeployment and operations: 10xgraph build writes a Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml and a Kubernetes manifest. The manifest's grace period and probe timeouts let in-flight agent runs finish during a rolling deploy. 10xgraph audit checks the environment and config before you ship, and it exits with code 1 on failure, so it can gate CI. Every response carries an X-Request-ID, and you can send errors to Sentry, traces to OpenTelemetry, and runs to Logfire or LangSmith.\n\nThe core is at 1.0.0rc4, the 1.0 release candidate, so pin your versions and read the changelog when you upgrade."
  },
  {
    q: "How does the server handle authentication and multi-user isolation?",
    a: "Authentication is part of the generated server. Use JWT or plug in your own auth class. The production template sets authorization to ownership, so a thread is accessible only to the user who created it, and owner checks are cached in process and in Redis so they do not cost a database round trip per request. You can also restrict individual tools by role."
  },
  {
    q: "What happens if the server crashes mid-run?",
    a: "With a checkpointer configured, 10xGraph records the current node before running it and keeps a ledger of every completed tool call. When the run resumes, on the same process or a new pod, the interrupted node runs again, but any tool call already in the ledger is skipped instead of executed. A payment tool that finished before the crash does not charge the card a second time."
  },
  {
    q: "How does my frontend talk to the server?",
    a: "Through the typed TypeScript client, installed with npm install 10xgraph-client. It covers invoke, streaming, threads, memory and file uploads, and it sends the same JWT the server checks. It works in Next.js, Vite and Node. Tools can also run in the browser, for work only the user's device can do."
  },
  {
    q: "How is 10xGraph different from LangGraph?",
    a: "Both model agents as graphs. With 10xGraph, the API server, authentication, rate limits and deployment files ship in the same MIT-licensed install, and you host them yourself. LangGraph offers its managed server through LangSmith Deployment. LangGraph also requires langchain-core, while 10xGraph has no LangChain dependency."
  },
  {
    q: "Which LLM providers does 10xGraph support?",
    a: "10xGraph picks the provider from the model string. It supports OpenAI, Google Gemini including Vertex AI, and Anthropic through the direct API, Vertex AI or Amazon Bedrock. Any OpenAI-compatible endpoint also works. Changing the model string does not change your graph, your tools or your API endpoints."
  },
  {
    q: "Is 10xGraph free and open source?",
    a: "Yes. 10xGraph is released under the MIT license, and that includes the API server and the CLI, not just the graph library. You can use it in commercial products, self-host it on your own infrastructure and modify it. There is no hosted platform to buy and no account to create before you start building."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I install 10xGraph?",
    a: "Run pip install 10xgraph 10xgraph-api on Python 3.12 or newer. Then run 10xgraph init --yes --template production to scaffold a project with auth, validators, tests and config, 10xgraph dev to serve it on port 8000 with the playground, and 10xgraph build --docker-compose --k8s to generate deployment files. The TypeScript client installs with npm install 10xgraph-client."
  },
  {
    q: "Was 10xGraph previously called Agentflow?",
    a: "Yes. 10xGraph was published as Agentflow, under the PyPI name 10xscale-agentflow, until 2026. The project was renamed because many unrelated tools share the Agentflow name. The Python package is now imported as tenxgraph. The old agentflow import path remains as a deprecated alias until 2.0, so existing code keeps working while you switch to the new package names."
  }
];
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "10xScale",
    "url": "https://10xscale.ai/"
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "WebSite",
    "name": "10xGraph",
    "url": "https://10xgraph.com/"
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    "name": "10xGraph",
    "alternateName": "Agentflow",
    "description": "10xGraph is an open-source Python framework for production multi-agent AI. You write the agent, and 10xGraph generates the production server around it: authentication, scoped access control, owner-only threads, rate limits, and Docker and Kubernetes deployment. Memory has two tiers, with hot data in a Redis cache and cold data in PostgreSQL. MIT licensed, made by 10xScale.",
    "applicationCategory": "DeveloperApplication",
    "operatingSystem": "Linux, macOS, Windows",
    "softwareVersion": "1.0.0rc4",
    "programmingLanguage": "Python",
    "license": "https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT",
    "url": "https://10xgraph.com/",
    "image": "https://10xgraph.com/logo-512.png",
    "codeRepository": "https://github.com/10xGraph/10xGraph",
    "offers": {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "price": "0",
      "priceCurrency": "USD"
    },
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "10xScale"
    }
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": faq.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": f.q,
      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": f.a }
    }))
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} exactTitle jsonLd={jsonLd}>
<div class="home">
<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero" aria-labelledby="h1">
  <div class="hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"></div>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Open source · MIT · Python 3.12+</p>
        <h1 id="h1">Write the Python agent. 10xGraph builds the <span class="hl">production server</span> around it.</h1>
        <p class="sub">Authentication (JWT or your own), authorization with owner-only threads, prompt-injection validators, rate limits, two-tier memory, replay-safe tools, and Docker and Kubernetes manifests come generated with your graph. MIT licensed and self-hosted, so the production layer is in the box.</p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/docs/get-started/installation">Build your first agent <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 8h9M8.5 4.5L12 8l-3.5 3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></a>
          <button class="pill" type="button" data-copy="pip install 10xgraph 10xgraph-api"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span><code>pip install 10xgraph 10xgraph-api</code><span class="cp" aria-live="polite">Copy</span></button>
        </div>
        <a class="link star" href="https://github.com/10xGraph/10xGraph">Star on GitHub</a>
      </div>

      <div class="stage" id="stage" role="region" aria-label="Animation: agent code unfolds into a running production system">
        <p class="sr-only">An editor types a short agent.py and a 10xgraph.json with JWT auth, ownership authorization and a Redis rate limit. The editor tilts into an isometric base, and the generated server rises on top of it in layers: input validators for prompt injection and jailbreaks, rate limits, authorization with RBAC and owner-only threads, authentication with JWT or a custom backend, and the API server on port 8000. Redis, PostgreSQL and Kubernetes pods slide in beside it. Requests from a TypeScript client travel down through the layers and a request log lists each one: an expired token gets 401 EXPIRED_TOKEN, another user's thread gets 403, a burst gets 429 with a retry-after, and an "ignore all previous instructions" message gets 422 prompt injection blocked. When a pod fails mid-refund, a new pod rises and the resumed run skips the refund because it is already in the ledger.</p>
        <div class="iso">
          <div class="iso-head" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="iso-live"><i></i><b id="phase">writing agent.py</b></span>
            <span class="iso-legend"><span><i class="k-you"></i>you write</span><span><i class="k-gen"></i>10xGraph generates</span><span><i class="k-inf"></i>your infra</span></span>
          </div>
          <div class="iso-scene" id="scene" aria-hidden="true">
            <div class="iso-tilt" id="tilt">
              <svg id="iso-svg" viewBox="0 0 640 430">
                <g class="iso-grid" id="iso-grid"></g>
                <g id="iso-world"></g>
                <g id="iso-labels"></g>
                <g id="iso-fx"></g>
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div class="iso-ed" id="iso-ed">
              <div class="iso-ed-tabs"><span id="iso-tab1" class="on">agent.py</span><span id="iso-tab2">10xgraph.json</span></div>
              <pre id="iso-pre"></pre>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="iso-log" aria-hidden="true"><ol id="iso-log"></ol></div>
          <div class="iso-foot">
            <div class="cmdl" aria-hidden="true"><span>$</span> <code id="iso-cmd"></code><i class="caret"></i></div>
            <span class="tag-ill">illustrative traffic</span>
            <button class="ctl" id="iso-ctl" type="button" aria-pressed="false"><svg class="i-pause" viewBox="0 0 10 10" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2.5 1.5v7M7.5 1.5v7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/></svg><svg class="i-play" viewBox="0 0 10 10" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M2.5 1.2l6 3.8-6 3.8z" fill="currentColor"/></svg><span class="ctl-t">Pause</span></button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="provider"><span class="eyebrow">Works with</span>OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, Vertex AI, Amazon Bedrock and any MCP server</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MOAT INDEX -->
<section class="moats" aria-label="What 10xGraph ships in the box">
  <div class="wrap">
    <ul class="moat-list">
      <li><a href="#in-the-box"><span>Production layer</span><b>In the box</b>Auth, permissions, rate limits and deploy files, generated.</a></li>
      <li><a href="#replay"><span>Reliability</span><b>Replay-safe tools</b>A crash and resume never runs a finished tool twice.</a></li>
      <li><a href="#memory"><span>Scale</span><b>Two-tier memory</b>Hot data in a Redis cache, cold data in PostgreSQL.</a></li>
      <li><a href="#stack"><span>Full stack</span><b>Python to TypeScript</b>Typed client, browser tools, realtime audio.</a></li>
      <li><a href="#yours"><span>Ownership</span><b>Yours to run</b>MIT, self-hosted, any model, no LangChain.</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHAT IS -->
<section aria-labelledby="what">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="def-grid">
      <div>
        <h2 id="what">What is 10xGraph?</h2>
        <p class="def">10xGraph is an open-source Python framework for production multi-agent AI. You write the agent, and 10xGraph generates the production server around it: authentication, scoped access control, owner-only threads, rate limits, and Docker and Kubernetes deployment. Memory has two tiers, with hot data in a Redis cache and cold data in PostgreSQL. MIT licensed, made by 10xScale.</p>
        <p class="fresh">Updated October 2026 · v1.0.0rc4 · <a href="/docs/concepts">Read the concepts</a></p>
      </div>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>License</dt><dd>MIT, including the API server and CLI. Use it commercially, self-host it, fork it.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Runtime</dt><dd>Python 3.12+. Core 1.0.0rc4, API and CLI 1.0.0rc4, TypeScript client 1.0.0-rc.2.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Memory</dt><dd>Hot: active thread state cached in Redis and read first. Cold: full history in PostgreSQL with versioned writes.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Dependencies</dt><dd>No LangChain. Dependency injection through InjectQ.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PILLAR 1 -->
<section id="in-the-box" aria-labelledby="p1h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker">Production layer, in the box</span>
        <h2 id="p1h">The production server ships with the framework</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:24px">Most agent frameworks stop at the graph. Serving it, securing it and deploying it becomes your project or a paid platform. 10xGraph generates that layer from your project config, under the same MIT license.</p>
        <ul class="points">
          <li><b>One install, one config file</b><code>pip install 10xgraph 10xgraph-api</code>, then <code>10xgraph.json</code> drives the server.</li>
          <li><b>Secure defaults</b>The production template turns on JWT auth, owner-only threads and a prompt-injection validator.</li>
          <li><b>Built for many users</b>Owner checks are cached in process and in Redis, so authorization does not cost a database round trip per request.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="cfg">
          <div class="cfg-head"><span>10xgraph.json key</span><span>what you get</span></div>
          <div class="cfg-row"><code>"auth": "jwt"</code><div><b>JWT authentication</b> on every endpoint, or plug in your own auth class.</div></div>
          <div class="cfg-row"><code>"authorization": "ownership"</code><div><b>Owner-only threads.</b> A thread is readable only by the user who created it.</div></div>
          <div class="cfg-row"><code>"rate_limit": &#123; "backend": "redis" &#125;</code><div><b>Rate limiting</b> shared across workers, or in memory for one process.</div></div>
          <div class="cfg-row"><code>build --docker-compose --k8s</code><div><b>Deployment files:</b> Dockerfile, docker-compose.yml and a Kubernetes Deployment and Service.</div></div>
          <div class="cfg-row"><code>POST /v1/graph/stream</code><div><b>Streaming</b> over SSE, plus WebSocket and a realtime audio endpoint.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="perm" aria-label="Access control example">
          <div class="cfg-head"><span>Scoped access</span><span class="tag-ill">illustrative</span></div>
          <div class="perm-row"><span>user <b>maya</b> · scopes <b>checkpointer:read</b> · calls <b>/v1/graph/invoke</b></span><span class="st no">403 denied</span></div>
          <div class="perm-row"><span>user <b>sam</b> · scopes <b>graph:invoke</b> · calls <b>/v1/graph/invoke</b></span><span class="st ok">200 allowed</span></div>
          <div class="perm-row"><span>user <b>maya</b> · reads a thread owned by <b>sam</b></span><span class="st no">403 denied</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="pillar-more"><a href="/docs/get-started/why-10xgraph">See everything the server does for you, and how to replace each part &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PILLAR 2 -->
<section id="replay" aria-labelledby="p2h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">Reliability</span>
    <h2 id="p2h">Crash mid-run. Resume without charging the card twice.</h2>
    <p class="lede">A process can die between two tool calls. When the run resumes, 10xGraph replays the interrupted step but checks a tool ledger first, so tools that already finished are skipped instead of executed again.</p>
    <div class="replay" role="group" aria-label="Replay example">
      <div class="lane">
        <div class="who">Run 1<small>thread order-1042</small></div>
        <div class="tc ran"><code>lookup_order</code><span>ran · recorded</span></div>
        <div class="tc ran"><code>charge_card</code><span>ran · recorded</span></div>
        <div class="tc kill"><code>send_receipt</code><span>process killed</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="lane">
        <div class="who">Resume<small>same thread</small></div>
        <div class="tc skip"><code>lookup_order</code><span>skipped · in ledger</span></div>
        <div class="tc skip"><code>charge_card</code><span>skipped · in ledger</span></div>
        <div class="tc ran"><code>send_receipt</code><span>ran · recorded</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="replay-foot">Requires a checkpointer. Ledger entries are keyed by the assistant message that issued the call plus the tool call id.</div>
    </div>
    <ul class="points" style="margin-top:32px">
      <li><b>Tool ledger</b>Each completed tool call is recorded as soon as it returns. Keys include the issuing message, so models that reuse ids like <code>call_1</code> never collide across turns.</li>
      <li><b>Versioned state writes</b>Durable writes use an optimistic version check, so two runs on the same thread cannot overwrite each other. The Redis cache is version-guarded too.</li>
      <li><b>Real timeouts</b><code>node_timeout</code> and <code>tool_timeout</code> stop a hung tool from holding a worker forever.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PILLAR 3 -->
<section id="memory" aria-labelledby="p3h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <span class="kicker">Scale</span>
    <h2 id="p3h">Two-tier memory that scales with your users</h2>
    <p class="lede">Active conversations need fast reads. History needs to be durable. 10xGraph keeps each kind of data in the store built for it.</p>
    <div class="tiers">
      <div class="tier hotc">
        <div class="lab"><span>Hot data</span><span>Redis cache</span></div>
        <h3>Active thread state</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Written on every step of a run and read first.</li>
          <li>Expires after 24 hours by default, configurable.</li>
          <li>Version-guarded writes, so a stale run cannot move state backwards.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="tier">
        <div class="lab"><span>Cold data</span><span>PostgreSQL</span></div>
        <h3>Durable history</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Full thread history with versioned writes.</li>
          <li>Recent state snapshots kept per thread (20 by default).</li>
          <li>Threads resume after restarts and after the cache expires.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p class="tier-flow"><span>Also included:</span><span>SQLite for local work</span><span>Qdrant and Mem0 for long-term memory</span><span>events to Kafka, Redis Pub/Sub, RabbitMQ, OpenTelemetry</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PILLAR 4 -->
<section id="stack" aria-labelledby="p4h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="two">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker">Full stack</span>
        <h2 id="p4h">One stack, Python backend to TypeScript frontend</h2>
        <ul class="points">
          <li><b>Typed TypeScript client</b>Invoke, stream, threads, memory and files. Works in Next.js, Vite and Node.</li>
          <li><b>Tools that run in the browser</b>Remote tools execute on the client, for things only the user's device can do, with validated schemas.</li>
          <li><b>Realtime audio</b>A WebSocket endpoint at <code>/v1/graph/live</code> for voice agents.</li>
          <li><b>Playground</b><code>10xgraph dev</code> starts the server and a React UI to chat with your agent.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="code"><div class="cap"><span>support-chat.ts</span><span>typescript</span></div><pre tabindex="0"><code><span class="k">import</span> &#123; TenxGraphClient, Message &#125; <span class="k">from</span> <span class="s">'10xgraph-client'</span>;

<span class="k">const</span> client = <span class="k">new</span> TenxGraphClient(&#123;
  baseUrl: <span class="s">'https://agents.example.com'</span>,
  authToken: userJwt,  <span class="c">// same JWT the API checks</span>
&#125;);

<span class="k">const</span> question = Message.text_message(<span class="s">'Where is order A-1042?'</span>, <span class="s">'user'</span>);

<span class="k">for await</span> (<span class="k">const</span> chunk <span class="k">of</span> client.stream([question])) &#123;
  <span class="k">if</span> (chunk.event === <span class="s">'message'</span>) render(chunk.message?.content);
&#125;</code></pre></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- PILLAR 5 -->
<section id="yours" aria-labelledby="p5h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="two" style="align-items:center">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker">Ownership</span>
        <h2 id="p5h">Yours to run, on any model</h2>
        <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:0">MIT licensed, self-hosted, no LangChain dependency. Pick the provider with one model string; the graph, tools and endpoints stay the same. 10xScale runs its own production AI products on 10xGraph.</p>
        <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Choose a provider" hidden>
          <button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-m="google/gemini-2.5-flash">Google</button>
          <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-m="openai/gpt-4o">OpenAI</button>
          <button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-m="anthropic/claude-opus-5">Anthropic</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="code"><div class="cap"><span>agent.py</span><span>python</span></div><pre tabindex="0"><code>agent = ReactAgent(
<span class="mline">    model=<span class="s" id="mstr">"google/gemini-2.5-flash"</span>,</span>    system_prompt=[&#123;<span class="s">"role"</span>: <span class="s">"system"</span>, <span class="s">"content"</span>: <span class="s">"You are a support agent."</span>&#125;],
    tools=[lookup_order],
)</code></pre><div class="same">same graph · same tools · same endpoints</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="own">
      <div class="limits">
        <h3>When 10xGraph is not the right choice</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>You need a large plugin marketplace today.</b> The community is smaller than LangGraph's or CrewAI's.</li>
          <li><b>You need a final 1.0 today.</b> 1.0 is a release candidate until the final ships. The stable-API policy applies from 1.0; pin versions and read the changelog.</li>
          <li><b>You want visual tooling first.</b> LangGraph Studio and LangSmith are stronger for visual debugging.</li>
          <li><b>You are on Python older than 3.12</b>, or you want a no-code builder.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="placeholder" role="note">
        <span class="tag">Placeholder: real user quote goes here</span>
        <p class="q">"Quote from a named engineer or team, with permission."</p>
        <span class="tag">Name, role, company</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- START HERE -->
<section aria-labelledby="start">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 id="start">Start here</h2>
    <span class="kicker">Learn directly from examples</span>
